## Proof

### CW structure of $\mathbb{CP}^n$

The complex projective space $\mathbb{CP}^n$ admits a CW decomposition with exactly one cell in each even dimension $0, 2, 4, \dots, 2n$ and no cells in odd dimensions. Concretely, the $2k$-skeleton is $\mathbb{CP}^k \subset \mathbb{CP}^n$, obtained by attaching a $2k$-cell to $\mathbb{CP}^{k-1}$ via the Hopf map $S^{2k-1} \to \mathbb{CP}^{k-1}$.

### Cellular chain complex and homology

The cellular chain complex $C_\bullet(\mathbb{CP}^n; \mathbb{Z})$ therefore has
$$C_k = \begin{cases} \mathbb{Z} & k = 0, 2, 4, \dots, 2n, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$

Every cellular boundary map $d_k : C_k \to C_{k-1}$ is zero: when $k$ is even, $C_{k-1} = 0$ (odd degree), and when $k$ is odd, $C_k = 0$. Hence the complex has trivial differentials throughout, and

$$\boxed{H_k(\mathbb{CP}^n; \mathbb{Z}) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{Z} & k = 0, 2, 4, \dots, 2n, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}}$$

### Cohomology

Since every homology group is free abelian, the Universal Coefficient Theorem gives, with no $\operatorname{Ext}$ correction term,

$$H^k(\mathbb{CP}^n; \mathbb{Z}) \cong \operatorname{Hom}(H_k(\mathbb{CP}^n), \mathbb{Z}) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{Z} & k = 0, 2, 4, \dots, 2n, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}$$

Moreover, the cohomology **ring** is
$$H^*(\mathbb{CP}^n; \mathbb{Z}) \cong \mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^{n+1}), \qquad |\alpha| = 2,$$
where $\alpha \in H^2(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{Z})$ is the generator (the first Chern class of the tautological line bundle, up to sign). The generator of $H^{2k}(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{Z})$ is $\alpha^k$.

### The inclusion $i : \mathbb{CP}^m \hookrightarrow \mathbb{CP}^n$, $m \le n$

Take the standard linear inclusion $[z_0 : \cdots : z_m] \mapsto [z_0 : \cdots : z_m : 0 : \cdots : 0]$. With respect to the CW structures above, this is a **cellular map**: it sends the $2k$-cell of $\mathbb{CP}^m$ homeomorphically onto the $2k$-cell of $\mathbb{CP}^n$ for $0 \le k \le m$, and $\mathbb{CP}^m$ has no cells in dimensions $> 2m$.

On the cellular chain level, the induced map $i_\# : C_\bullet(\mathbb{CP}^m) \to C_\bullet(\mathbb{CP}^n)$ is the identity $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z}$ in each even degree $0, 2, \dots, 2m$ and zero elsewhere. Since all differentials vanish, passing to homology gives:

$$\boxed{i_* : H_k(\mathbb{CP}^m;\mathbb{Z}) \to H_k(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{Z}) \text{ is } \begin{cases} \text{an isomorphism} & k = 0, 2, \dots, 2m, \\ 0 \text{ (trivially, domain } = 0) & k > 2m.\end{cases}}$$

Dually, on cohomology $i^* : H^*(\mathbb{CP}^n) \to H^*(\mathbb{CP}^m)$:

$$i^* : H^k(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{Z}) \to H^k(\mathbb{CP}^m;\mathbb{Z}) \text{ is } \begin{cases} \text{an isomorphism} & k = 0, 2, \dots, 2m, \\ 0 \text{ (trivially, codomain } = 0) & k > 2m.\end{cases}$$

### Ring-level behavior of $i^*$

The ring structure is preserved: if $\alpha_n \in H^2(\mathbb{CP}^n)$ and $\alpha_m \in H^2(\mathbb{CP}^m)$ denote the respective degree-2 generators, then $i^*(\alpha_n) = \alpha_m$, and consequently
$$i^*(\alpha_n^k) = \alpha_m^k, \qquad 0 \le k \le m.$$
For $k > m$, $\alpha_n^k$ maps to $\alpha_m^k = 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}[\alpha_m]/(\alpha_m^{m+1})$, consistent with $H^{2k}(\mathbb{CP}^m) = 0$ for $k > m$. In other words, $i^*$ is the natural quotient map
$$\mathbb{Z}[\alpha_n]/(\alpha_n^{n+1}) \longrightarrow \mathbb{Z}[\alpha_m]/(\alpha_m^{m+1}), \qquad \alpha_n \mapsto \alpha_m.$$

### Summary

- **Homology and cohomology groups** (with $\mathbb{Z}$ coefficients) of $\mathbb{CP}^n$ are $\mathbb{Z}$ in every even degree $0, 2, \dots, 2n$ and $0$ in every odd degree (and above $2n$).
- **Cohomology ring**: $H^*(\mathbb{CP}^n;\mathbb{Z}) \cong \mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^{n+1})$, $|\alpha|=2$.
- **Inclusion $i : \mathbb{CP}^m \hookrightarrow \mathbb{CP}^n$ ($m \le n$)**: $i_*$ on homology and $i^*$ on cohomology are isomorphisms in degrees $0, 2, \dots, 2m$, and zero (for trivial reasons) above degree $2m$. At the ring level $i^*$ is the surjection $\mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^{n+1}) \twoheadrightarrow \mathbb{Z}[\alpha]/(\alpha^{m+1})$ sending $\alpha \mapsto \alpha$.

